# Multi-Hop QA

In this notebook you use `:gepa` to improve a two-step program. The first
step selects evidence passages and the second answers from that evidence.
The metric returns a score and short feedback, GEPA sends that feedback to a
proposal model, and the proposal model rewrites one instruction at a time.
The notebook runs offline with no credentials.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"},
  {:plug, "~> 1.20"}
])
```

## Script the models

`Scripted` serves OpenAI-compatible replies through an in-process Plug, so
ReqLLM runs its real request path without a network call. Each role gets a
reply function of the decoded request body, because GEPA's request order
depends on the search. In real use you pass a real model spec and the host's
credentials instead.

```elixir
defmodule Scripted do
  @moduledoc "Serves scripted OpenAI-compatible replies to ReqLLM through an in-process Plug."

  def model, do: %{provider: :openai, id: "gpt-4-turbo", extra: %{wire: %{protocol: "openai_chat"}}}

  def options(reply) when is_function(reply, 1) do
    plug = fn conn ->
      {:ok, body, conn} = Plug.Conn.read_body(conn)
      respond(conn, reply.(JSON.decode!(body)))
    end

    [api_key: "scripted", req_http_options: [plug: plug], max_retries: 0]
  end

  def role(reply), do: [model: model(), adapter: Tune.Adapter.Chat, request_options: options(reply)]

  def chat(fields) do
    Enum.map_join(fields, "\n\n", fn {name, value} ->
      text = if is_binary(value), do: value, else: JSON.encode!(value)
      "[[ ## #{name} ## ]]\n#{text}"
    end) <> "\n\n[[ ## completed ## ]]"
  end

  def system(body), do: hd(body["messages"])["content"]

  def field(body, name) do
    text = body["messages"] |> Enum.filter(&(&1["role"] == "user")) |> List.last() |> Map.fetch!("content")

    case Regex.run(~r/\[\[ ## #{name} ## \]\]\n(.*?)(?=\n\n\[\[ ## |\n\nRespond with|\z)/s, text) do
      [_, value] -> value
      nil -> nil
    end
  end

  defp respond(conn, text) do
    send_json(conn, 200, %{
      id: "scripted",
      model: "gpt-4-turbo",
      choices: [%{message: %{role: "assistant", content: text}, finish_reason: "stop"}],
      usage: %{prompt_tokens: 10, completion_tokens: 5, total_tokens: 15}
    })
  end

  defp send_json(conn, status, body) do
    conn
    |> Plug.Conn.put_resp_content_type("application/json")
    |> Plug.Conn.send_resp(status, JSON.encode!(body))
  end
end
```

## Define the program

`MultiHop` implements `Tune.Program`. It declares two predictors, `"select"`
and `"answer"`, and composes them in plain Elixir. GEPA treats each
predictor's instruction as a separate component.

```elixir
defmodule MultiHop do
  @moduledoc "Selects evidence passages, then answers from the selected evidence."
  @behaviour Tune.Program

  alias Tune.{Execution, Predictor, Signature}

  defstruct [:select, :answer]

  @passage Zoi.object(%{"id" => Zoi.string(), "text" => Zoi.string()})

  def new do
    {:ok, select} =
      Predictor.new(
        signature: [
          instructions: "Select the passages that help answer the question.",
          inputs: [context: Zoi.array(@passage), question: Zoi.string()],
          outputs: [evidence: Zoi.array(@passage)]
        ]
      )

    {:ok, answer} =
      Predictor.new(
        signature: [
          instructions: "Answer the question from the evidence. Cite the passage ids you used.",
          inputs: [question: Zoi.string(), evidence: Zoi.array(@passage)],
          outputs: [answer: Zoi.string(), sources: Zoi.array(Zoi.string())]
        ]
      )

    %__MODULE__{select: select, answer: answer}
  end

  @impl true
  def signature(_program) do
    {:ok, signature} =
      Signature.new(
        inputs: [context: Zoi.array(@passage), question: Zoi.string()],
        outputs: [answer: Zoi.string(), sources: Zoi.array(Zoi.string())]
      )

    signature
  end

  @impl true
  def parameters(program), do: %{"select" => program.select, "answer" => program.answer}

  @impl true
  def put_parameters(program, parameters),
    do: %{program | select: parameters["select"], answer: parameters["answer"]}

  @impl true
  def call(_program, inputs, execution) do
    with {:ok, %{"evidence" => evidence}, execution} <- Execution.predict(execution, "select", inputs) do
      Execution.predict(execution, "answer", %{"question" => inputs["question"], "evidence" => evidence})
    end
  end
end
```

## Prepare cases and feedback

Two questions need two hops: the bridge's designer, then the designer's
birthplace. A third question needs only the opening hours. Training and
validation use the same three questions with different IDs.

The metric returns `%{score: ..., feedback: ...}`. The feedback names the
missing passage or the unsupported answer.

```elixir
rows = fn split ->
  two_hop =
    for {bridge, designer, country} <- [{"Pine Bridge", "Nora Hill", "Canada"}, {"Copper Bridge", "Tomas Velez", "Chile"}] do
      %{
        id: "#{split}-#{bridge}",
        input: %{
          question: "Which country was the designer of #{bridge} born in? (#{split})",
          context: [
            %{"id" => "design", "text" => "#{bridge} was designed by #{designer}."},
            %{"id" => "birth", "text" => "#{designer} was born in #{country}."},
            %{"id" => "hours", "text" => "#{bridge} is open from dawn to dusk."}
          ]
        },
        expected: %{answer: country, sources: ["design", "birth"]}
      }
    end

  one_hop = %{
    id: "#{split}-Stone Bridge",
    input: %{
      question: "When is Stone Bridge open? (#{split})",
      context: [
        %{"id" => "design", "text" => "Stone Bridge was designed by Ada Park."},
        %{"id" => "birth", "text" => "Ada Park was born in Peru."},
        %{"id" => "hours", "text" => "Stone Bridge is open from 6am to 10pm."}
      ]
    },
    expected: %{answer: "from 6am to 10pm", sources: ["hours"]}
  }

  two_hop ++ [one_hop]
end

grounded = fn row, output ->
  missing = row.expected["sources"] -- output["sources"]

  cond do
    output["answer"] == row.expected["answer"] and missing == [] ->
      %{score: 1, feedback: "The cited evidence supports the answer."}

    missing != [] ->
      %{score: 0, feedback: "missing evidence: the answer needs passage #{Enum.join(missing, " and ")}"}

    true ->
      %{score: 0, feedback: "unsupported answer: the cited passages say #{row.expected["answer"]}, not #{output["answer"]}"}
  end
end

:ok
```

## Script the target and the reflector

The scripted target follows its instructions literally:

- By default, the selector drops the birthplace passage for Pine Bridge, and
  the answerer guesses "Argentina" for Copper Bridge.
- "Retain both hops" keeps the designer and birthplace passages for every
  question, including the opening-hours question.
- "Keep every passage the question needs" keeps the right passages for each
  question.
- "Connect both passages" makes the answerer read the birthplace from the
  second passage.

The scripted reflector reads the feedback GEPA sends. For the selector, it
proposes a general rule when the trajectories include the opening-hours
question and a two-hop rule otherwise. It records each request so you can
inspect the evidence later.

```elixir
select = fn body ->
  question = Scripted.field(body, "question")
  instruction = Scripted.system(body)
  [design, birth, hours] = JSON.decode!(Scripted.field(body, "context"))
  needed = if question =~ "open", do: [hours], else: [design, birth]

  evidence =
    cond do
      instruction =~ "Keep every passage the question needs" -> needed
      instruction =~ "Retain both hops" -> [design, birth]
      question =~ "Pine" -> [design]
      true -> needed
    end

  Scripted.chat(evidence: evidence)
end

answer = fn body ->
  question = Scripted.field(body, "question")
  instruction = Scripted.system(body)
  evidence = JSON.decode!(Scripted.field(body, "evidence"))
  ids = Enum.map(evidence, & &1["id"])
  two_hop? = not (question =~ "open") and ids == ["design", "birth"]

  cond do
    question =~ "open" and ids == ["hours"] ->
      [_, hours] = Regex.run(~r/open (.+)\.$/, hd(evidence)["text"])
      Scripted.chat(answer: hours, sources: ids)

    two_hop? and (question =~ "Pine" or instruction =~ "Connect both passages") ->
      [_, country] = Regex.run(~r/born in (.+)\.$/, List.last(evidence)["text"])
      Scripted.chat(answer: country, sources: ids)

    two_hop? ->
      Scripted.chat(answer: "Argentina", sources: ids)

    true ->
      Scripted.chat(answer: "unknown", sources: ids)
  end
end

target = fn body -> if Scripted.field(body, "context"), do: select.(body), else: answer.(body) end

{:ok, reflections} = Agent.start_link(fn -> [] end)

reflector = fn body ->
  feedback = Scripted.field(body, "examples_with_feedback")
  selector? = Scripted.field(body, "task") =~ "Outputs: `evidence`"

  instruction =
    cond do
      selector? and feedback =~ "open?" -> "Keep every passage the question needs."
      selector? -> "Retain both hops: the designer and the birthplace."
      true -> "Connect both passages and cite their IDs."
    end

  Agent.update(reflections, &(&1 ++ [%{proposed: instruction, feedback: feedback}]))
  Scripted.chat(new_instruction: instruction)
end

study =
  Tune.new(MultiHop.new(), model: Scripted.role(target), proposal: Scripted.role(reflector))
  |> Tune.data(train: rows.("practice"), validation: rows.("holdout"))
  |> Tune.score(:grounded, grounded)
  |> Tune.search(:gepa, max_iterations: 8, minibatch_size: 2, use_merge: false)
  |> Tune.limit(seed: 2, model_calls: 150)

:ok
```

## Find the failing cases

Evaluate the original program on the validation split.

```elixir
{:ok, baseline} = Tune.evaluate(study, :validation)

for observation <- baseline.observations do
  IO.puts("#{observation.id}: #{observation.scores.grounded} - #{observation.feedback.grounded}")
end

:ok
```

```text
holdout-Pine Bridge: 0 - missing evidence: the answer needs passage birth
holdout-Copper Bridge: 0 - unsupported answer: the cited passages say Chile, not Argentina
holdout-Stone Bridge: 1 - The cited evidence supports the answer.
```

Pine Bridge fails in the selector, Copper Bridge fails in the answerer, and
Stone Bridge already passes.

## Run GEPA

Each iteration picks a parent from the per-case Pareto frontier, runs it on a
training minibatch, and asks the proposal model to rewrite one instruction
from those trajectories. The child must beat its parent on the same
minibatch before it gets a full validation pass.

```elixir
{:ok, winner} = Tune.tune(study)

IO.inspect(winner.best.evaluation.selected_score, label: "validation score")
IO.inspect(winner.calls, label: "calls")

IO.puts("select: " <> winner.value.select.signature.instructions)
IO.puts("answer: " <> winner.value.answer.signature.instructions)
```

```text
validation score: 1.0
calls: %{target: 82, proposal: 8}
select: Keep every passage the question needs.
answer: Connect both passages and cite their IDs.
```

Both instructions changed. The proposal calls count against the same
`model_calls` limit as the target calls.

## Read the evidence behind a change

The reflector's request holds the trajectories and feedback that produced
the selector instruction in the winner.

```elixir
reflections
|> Agent.get(& &1)
|> Enum.find(&(&1.proposed == "Keep every passage the question needs."))
|> Map.fetch!(:feedback)
|> String.split("\n")
|> Enum.filter(&String.starts_with?(&1, ["score:", "feedback:"]))
|> Enum.each(&IO.puts/1)
```

```text
score: 1
feedback: The cited evidence supports the answer.
score: 0
feedback: missing evidence: the answer needs passage hours
```

The parent was a candidate whose selector said "Retain both hops". It passed
Pine Bridge, but it dropped the hours passage for the opening-hours
question. Each trajectory in the request also includes the case inputs and
every step's inputs and outputs, so the reflector sees which step lost the
passage.

## Compare candidates by case

`winner.history` holds every candidate that reached validation, with its
per-case scores.

```elixir
for candidate <- winner.history do
  scores =
    Enum.map_join(candidate.evaluation.observations, " ", fn observation ->
      "#{String.replace_prefix(observation.id, "holdout-", "")}=#{observation.scores.grounded}"
    end)

  IO.puts("#{candidate.id} #{candidate.metadata.label} #{candidate.metadata[:component]}: #{scores}")
  IO.puts("  select: #{candidate.value.select.signature.instructions}")
end

:ok
```

```text
0 baseline : Pine Bridge=0 Copper Bridge=0 Stone Bridge=1
  select: Select the passages that help answer the question.
1 reflect select: Pine Bridge=1 Copper Bridge=0 Stone Bridge=0
  select: Retain both hops: the designer and the birthplace.
2 reflect answer: Pine Bridge=1 Copper Bridge=1 Stone Bridge=0
  select: Retain both hops: the designer and the birthplace.
3 reflect answer: Pine Bridge=0 Copper Bridge=1 Stone Bridge=1
  select: Select the passages that help answer the question.
4 reflect select: Pine Bridge=1 Copper Bridge=1 Stone Bridge=1
  select: Keep every passage the question needs.
```

Candidate 1 fixes Pine Bridge and breaks Stone Bridge: "Retain both hops"
drops the opening-hours passage. Its mean equals the baseline mean. GEPA
still adds it to the pool, and because it has the best score on Pine Bridge it
stays on the per-case Pareto frontier and can be chosen as a parent. An
average alone would have hidden that strength. Candidate 4 starts
from candidate 2 and replaces the two-hop rule with one that works for both
kinds of question.

The iteration log also records children that did not reach validation.

```elixir
winner.metadata.notes.iterations
|> Enum.reject(& &1.accepted?)
|> Enum.map(&{&1.iteration, &1.parent, &1.component, &1.parent_minibatch, &1.child_minibatch})
```

Each tuple is `{iteration, parent, component, parent_minibatch, child_minibatch}`:

```text
[
  {1, "0", "answer", 1, 1},
  {4, "2", "answer", 1, nil},
  {5, "1", "select", 1, nil},
  {8, "3", "answer", 1, nil}
]
```

In iteration 1 the child tied its parent on the minibatch, so it was not
validated. Here, each `nil` child score means the proposal repeated the current
instruction, so there was nothing new to score. A `nil` also marks a proposal
that fails validation or arrives after the budget is spent.

